; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Recursive Spirals
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     recursion, spirals, drawing, stop rule, color
; SUMMARY:  Draws four colorful spirals with one short procedure that draws a single line and then calls itself with a shorter one.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   SPIRAL draws one line, turns, and then asks itself to draw the rest
;   of the spiral with a slightly shorter line. The stop rule says:
;   when the line is shorter than 2 steps, stop. Four spirals are
;   drawn with different turning angles, one in each corner, and the
;   pen color changes as the lines get shorter.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and watch. Move the speed slider to the left to slow
;      the turtle down so you can see each line being added.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line  fd :size  inside SPIRAL and run.
;      Each time you press F5 one more line appears, and :size in the
;      Variables panel is a little smaller.
;   3. After many presses, open the Call Stack. It is very tall: none
;      of the SPIRALs finish until the very last, tiny one stops.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a stop rule that tests the size, not a counter
;   * changing more than one input on each recursive call
;   * tail recursion: the recursive call is the very last thing
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Try angles like 89, 90, 121, 160 or 170 in the last lines.
;   * Make the line shrink by 1 instead of by 4 percent. What changes?
;   * Make it GROW instead and add a stop rule for when it gets too big.
; ============================================================

; Each call: one line in a color picked from the size, a turn, then the
; rest of the spiral, which is the same picture made 4 percent smaller.
to spiral :size :angle
  if :size < 2 [stop]
  setpencolor colorfor :size
  fd :size
  rt :angle
  spiral :size * 0.96 :angle
end

; Big lines get a cool blue, small ones a warm red: the color slides
; smoothly between them as the size shrinks.
to colorfor :size
  localmake "t :size / 130
  if :t > 1 [make "t 1]
  output (list 255 * (1 - :t) 80 255 * :t)
end

; Start each spiral at a point chosen so that it sits roughly in the
; middle of its own quarter of the screen.
to spiral.at :x :y :angle
  penup setxy :x :y setheading 0 pendown
  spiral 130 :angle
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground 0
setpensize 2
; Angles a little past 90, 120, 72 and 144 make the polygons twist
spiral.at -258 85 92
spiral.at 162 85 123
spiral.at -290 -215 74
spiral.at 179 -215 146
